<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter11_exceptions-in-functions-and-classes">
    <title>Exceptions in Functions and Classes</title>

    <p>
        A function or member function should protect the requirements of its
        interface. When an argument would make the requested operation invalid,
        the function may throw an exception instead of creating an invalid
        result.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_function-contracts-and-exceptions">
        <title>Exceptions and Function Contracts</title>

        <p>
            A function that throws should make the possibility visible in its
            documentation and should use an exception type that describes the
            failure. The caller can then decide whether to recover, report the
            problem, or allow it to propagate farther.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_function-contract">
            <title>Protecting a function's precondition</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_function-contract"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

double calculate_average(int total, int count) {
    if (count <= 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("count must be positive");
    }

    return static_cast<double>(total) / count;
}

int main() {
    try {
        std::cout << calculate_average(85, 0) << '\n';
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument& error) {
        std::cout << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The function either returns a valid average or reports that its
            precondition was violated. It does not return a misleading value
            such as zero to represent every possible failure.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_constructor-exceptions">
        <title>Constructors Can Reject Invalid Objects</title>

        <p>
            A constructor may throw when its arguments cannot create a valid
            object. This protects the class invariant: an object whose
            constructor finishes successfully satisfies the class's validity
            rules.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_constructor-exception">
            <title>Rejecting an invalid temperature</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_constructor-exception"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

class Temperature {
private:
    double celsius;

public:
    Temperature(double starting_celsius)
        : celsius{starting_celsius} {
        if (celsius < -273.15) {
            throw std::invalid_argument(
                "temperature is below absolute zero"
            );
        }
    }

    double get_celsius() const {
        return celsius;
    }
};

int main() {
    try {
        Temperature temperature{-300.0};
        std::cout << temperature.get_celsius() << '\n';
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument& error) {
        std::cout << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            Because construction fails, the caller does not receive a usable
            <c>Temperature</c> object. The class does not need to provide an
            invalid object and hope that every later member function remembers
            to check it.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_function-class-visualization">
        <title>Tracing Constructor Validation</title>

        <p>
            The static listing shows that the constructor detects the invalid temperature before a usable <c>Temperature</c> object becomes available. Control then moves to the handler.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter11_functions-exercise-precondition">
        <statement><p>Why does <c>calculate_average</c> throw when <c>count</c> is zero?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                            <statement><p><c>count</c> must be negative.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>The function requires a positive count, so zero and negative counts violate the precondition.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The compiler cannot divide any integers.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>C++ can divide integers; division by zero is the invalid situation.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The function has no return statement.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The function has a return statement for valid input.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Its precondition requires a positive count.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The function cannot calculate a valid average with zero values.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>All averages must be integers.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The function returns a <c>double</c>; the issue is the count.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_functions-parsons-constructor">
        <statement><p>Arrange the constructor so that it rejects an invalid object.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>Temperature(double starting_celsius)</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>: celsius{starting_celsius} {</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>if (celsius &lt; -273.15) {</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>throw std::invalid_argument("temperature is invalid");</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>}</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_functions-exercise-constructor">
        <statement><p>What does a constructor exception prevent?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It makes the constructor return a Boolean.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Constructors do not return values; they either create an object or throw.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It prevents every object of the class from ever being created.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Valid constructor arguments can still create objects.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>It constructs and returns the invalid object anyway.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>A constructor exception prevents an invalid object from being made available.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It changes a class into a structure.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Exceptions do not change the kind of user-defined type.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>It prevents the caller from receiving an object that violates the class invariant.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A constructor that finishes successfully establishes a valid object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_functions-exercise-design">
        <statement><p>Which design best protects a class invariant?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>Make invariant checks optional for callers.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>An invariant is a class responsibility, not merely a suggestion to callers.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Reject invalid constructor arguments before the object becomes available.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Preventing invalid objects simplifies every later member function.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Remove all private data members.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Private data helps a class protect its invariant.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Accept every value and ask callers to remember the invariant.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>That spreads the responsibility across every caller.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Silently replace every invalid value with zero.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Silently changing input may hide an important programming error.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_functions-exercise-invariants">
        <statement>
            <p>Select all practices that help a class protect its invariant.</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Make all data members public and rely on callers to behave correctly.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Public unrestricted data makes invariant protection harder.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Validate constructor arguments before the object becomes available.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Rejecting invalid initial state protects every later operation.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Silently accept every invalid value and document the problem later.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Silently accepting invalid state weakens the class invariant.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Reject an invalid operation before it leaves the object in an unacceptable state.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Operations should preserve the invariant as well as constructors.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Remove validation so every constructor call succeeds.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Successful construction should mean that the object is valid.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
